Id: boot008w
Severity: WARN
Category: boot
Check: check_lilo
Fix: Set a superuser and password (grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2) in /etc/grub.d/40_custom and regenerate; on Red Hat's systems run grub2-setpassword.
References: https://www.gnu.org/software/grub/manual/grub/html_node/Authentication-and-authorisation.html; grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2(1); grub-mkconfig(8); grub2-setpassword(8)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-3, CM-6; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

GRUB is not configured with a password, so anyone at the console can
edit the boot entry (init=/bin/sh, single) and get a root shell. Set a
superuser and a password with grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2 in
/etc/grub.d/40_custom and regenerate the configuration. On a machine
without console access by untrusted people this may be acceptable.

GRUB 2 asks for a password only when "set superusers=" names someone: a
password or password_pbkdf2 line without it protects nothing, and is
reported as such. The configuration is read in /boot/grub (Debian,
Ubuntu, Arch) and /boot/grub2 (Fedora, RHEL, SUSE), with the custom.cfg
beside it. On Red Hat's systems grub2-setpassword writes the password to
user.cfg, and the 01_users block of grub.cfg sets superusers and the
password only when that file has GRUB2_PASSWORD: without it, the block
is there and protects nothing.
